Health Checks

Every 500ms the fast beat asks each local workload one question: are
you alive? The answer comes from exactly one of three probes, chosen
per workload. Healthy answers write nothing anywhere; sickness edges
write one journal event (signal action) or restart the container
(restart action). Raw observations stay in memory; only the edge
converges. The beat mechanics live in docs/metrics.

The three probes

  http   GET http://127.0.0.1:host-portpath   2xx means healthy
  tcp    dial 127.0.0.1:host-port               connect means healthy
  exec   run argv inside the container            exit code zero means healthy

Configure on submit; empty probe means HTTP, so every old spec behaves
exactly as before:

  # HTTP (default): needs mapped ports and an endpoint
  concord workload run -p 8080:80 --health-path /health nginx:alpine

  # TCP: needs mapped ports, speaks no HTTP
  concord workload run -p 1883:1883 --health-probe tcp mosquitto:alpine

  # Exec: needs a command as one string, split on whitespace.
  // Below runs /bin/check --ready inside the container.
  concord workload run --health-probe exec \
    --health-cmd "/bin/check --ready" \
    mydriver:latest

Rules that bite

Misconfiguration is loud, never silent. Unknown probe names, TCP
without a mapped host port, and exec without a command all report
unhealthy: restart loops and signal edges surface the mistake instead
of hiding it. One exception, kept for old specs: HTTP without mapped
ports skips the check as healthy.

Probes are bounded. Every probe carries a 5s cap (HTTP client,
TCP dial, exec run with SIGKILL escalation), matching the beat's
existing latency profile. Exec output discards into nothing: probe
chatter stays out of log rings by design.

Exec runs inside the container (namespaces joined, working dir /,
spec env plus a default PATH), not on the host. An empty environment
still resolves bare binary names. The container handle serves exec
only; adopted containers probe normally from reattach on.

Actions on sickness

When a check fails, the workload's configured action runs. There are
two of them.

Restart stops the container and starts it again, waiting longer
between tries each time it keeps failing. Use it for things that
crash: a dead driver should come back fresh.

Signal leaves the container running and writes one note into the
shared journal saying this workload is sick. Exactly one note per
sickness: nothing more until the workload recovers, then it may write
again. Use it for things that limp: a slow inference server should be
flagged, not killed.

Probes changed none of this. They only decide how sickness is
detected; the action decides what happens next.

Where it lives

Probe selection (HealthProbe in internal/workload/workload.go),
checks (CheckHealth plus per-probe helpers in
internal/cr/health.go), the beat (runHealthChecks in
internal/reconciler/fastpath.go), flags (--health-probe,
--health-cmd in internal/cli/workload.go), SDK builder
(HealthProbe in sdk/builder.go, mirrored types in sdk/types.go),
translation both ways (internal/ipc/handlers.go).
